---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro'
import { LINKS, SITE } from '../../consts'

/*
 * The canonical answer for support and issue triage. Written to be linked to
 * rather than improvised in a reply, the TickBox judgment rested on a
 * project's promotional and customer-support conduct, not on its code.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Acceptable use"
  description="What Playarr Server is for, what this project will not help with, and where responsibility sits for the media an operator stores and shares."
>
  <Section eyebrow="Legal" title="Acceptable use">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        This page is short on purpose, and it is the project's settled position rather than one
        maintainer's opinion on a given day.
      </p>

      <h2>What {SITE.name} is</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is a media server. It catalogues media that is already present on storage you
        control, and streams it to client applications you run. That is the entirety of what it
        does.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is not</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} supplies no media and ships nothing preconfigured that points at any external
        source. The server itself performs no indexing and obtains no media itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a user with request rights asks for a title that is not in the library, Playarr
        Server passes the request on. It goes either to a request manager (an Ombi or Seerr style
        application) that the operator has configured or, if none is configured, directly to the
        operator's own library manager (Radarr or Sonarr), asking it to add the title and look
        for it. Whether and how those applications search for or acquire anything is set up and
        controlled by the operator, under the operator's responsibility. Playarr itself obtains
        no media.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operators who do not want this can withhold the request permission. It is off by default
        for new accounts, administrators can always request, and the
        <code>PLAYARR_REQUESTS_ALLOW_ALL_USERS</code> setting, which grants it to every account,
        is off unless an operator sets it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where it reads from library-management applications, it reads only what those applications
        have already organised on your own storage, titles, file paths and metadata identifiers.
      </p>

      <h2>Your responsibility</h2>
      <p>
        You are responsible for the legality of what you store, play and share using this software,
        under the law that applies to you. Running a server for other people, or joining servers
        together, does not move that responsibility elsewhere. If you are unsure whether something
        you hold may lawfully be shared with a particular person, the answer is to find out before
        you share it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You are also responsible for ensuring that every person you invite is allowed to access
        the media. Do not use {SITE.name} to infringe rights, evade access controls, distribute
        unlawful material, harm others or interfere with the service.
      </p>

      <h2>What this project will not help with</h2>
      <p>
        Maintainers will not assist with obtaining media, will not discuss sources of media, and
        will not accept contributions, documentation, issues or feature requests directed at those
        ends. Requests of that kind will be closed with a link to this page. This is not
        squeamishness, it is the line that keeps a neutral tool a neutral tool.
      </p>

      <h2>Enforcement</h2>
      <p>
        The project may reject support requests, and may block hosted-service use, that violates
        this policy, threatens the service or creates legal or security risk.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting a concern</h2>
      <p>
        Rights holders and anyone reporting abuse can write to
        <a href="mailto:support@playarr.app">support@playarr.app</a>. For a security concern you
        can also <a href={LINKS.issues} rel="noopener">open an issue</a>, without including
        private details.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please include the work concerned, where it appears, your contact details, and a statement
        that you believe in good faith that the use is not authorised.
      </p>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} hosts no content. Media on a self-hosted server is stored and shared by that
        server's operator, so complaints about it should go to the operator of that server. We
        will respond to what falls within the project's control.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        This software is provided “as is”, without warranty of any kind, as set out in the
        <a href="/legal/licences">MIT licence</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
  }
</style>
